WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420
- [Reporter] COVID-19 has caused a surge

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:07.260
in global unemployment like
we've never seen before.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:10.470
But in the U.S. jobs
have disappeared faster

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:12.913
than in any other developed country.

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:18.900
And then in May America's
unemployment rate began to rebound

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:20.790
while in other developed nations,

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:22.810
it remained relatively stable.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:24.760
- The U.S. also creates a ton of jobs

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:27.550
because it's easier to fire people.

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:30.060
Businesses are more
confident to hire people.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.990
So you'd expect that the
flexible labor markets

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:37.990
to move more, I suppose, in
response to changes in demand.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.370
- [Reporter] In May and June,

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:43.990
the U.S. added about 7.5 million jobs

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:46.920
but the unemployment rate is
still at the highest level

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.910
since the depression.

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:51.570
As the pandemic has put
us in uncharted territory,

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:53.850
we wanted to know, will American jobs

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.323
bounce back faster than
the rest of the world?

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.220
First, it's important to understand

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.800
what difference a flexible
labor market can make.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.500
- In Europe, there's more of a sense that

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.040
the company has to have
a very strong reason

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:08.660
to be able to fire someone,
whereas in the U.S.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:11.423
I think it's much more of a
normal part of a business.

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:15.510
- [Reporter] A flexible labor
market has its pros and cons

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:17.880
and proponents say in normal times,

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:19.650
it helps keep unemployment low

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:22.920
by making it easy for
people to move between jobs.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:23.900
Just look at the difference

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:25.410
in how long people stay at their jobs

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:27.144
in the U.S. versus Germany.

00:01:27.144 --> 00:01:29.477
- It's a different
mentality that in Germany,

00:01:29.477 --> 00:01:30.647
it's not a job for life,

00:01:30.647 --> 00:01:33.490
but the average employment
tenure is 10 years

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:35.770
at a single company, compared
with 4.2, I think it is,

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:36.857
in the U S.

00:01:36.857 --> 00:01:39.520
- [Reporter] After the
2008 financial crisis,

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:43.030
the U S unemployment rate
recovered faster than Europe's,

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:45.950
which also went through a debt crisis.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:48.550
So, it makes sense that a
more flexible labor market

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:51.550
would see a bigger jump in
unemployment during a recession,

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:54.150
since it's easier for
companies to lay off employees

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:55.710
when demand falls.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:57.130
But if you look at what happened

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:58.550
at the start of the pandemic,

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:00.560
you'll see two very different outcomes

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.650
for two job markets
economist deemed flexible.

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:05.740
The U.S. saw unemployment rise,

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:09.470
roughly 11 percentage
points from January to April

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:11.420
while Japan's unemployment rate

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:13.880
grew half a percentage point.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:16.803
That's where different
pandemic strategies come in.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:21.110
In short, the U.S. chose to
give money directly to workers

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:23.130
while the economy reboots,

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:25.700
while others like Japan and Europe

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.770
opted to subsidize
companies and protect jobs.

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:31.220
- Canada and the U.S. I think
have been among the ones

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.910
that have seen the biggest jump.

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:35.430
The main reason I suppose, is because

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:39.320
they've tried to protect
workers rather than jobs.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:42.422
They haven't gone for
these job furlough schemes

00:02:42.422 --> 00:02:47.180
on a grand scale as the Europeans have.

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:49.740
- [Reporter] As part of
its first stimulus package,

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:54.010
lawmakers sent Americans a
$1,200 one time stimulus cheque

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:57.850
and added $600 a week to
unemployment benefits,

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:01.130
effectively accepting
a wave of job losses.

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:03.400
They also funded key
industries like aviation

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:06.200
to try and prevent or delay some layoffs.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:10.160
Meanwhile, Japan's government
allocated nearly $700 billion

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:11.710
to business financing

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:13.290
and enacted a program

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:16.920
to subsidize up to around
$140 a day in wages

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.220
for furloughed employees.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:22.760
The EU has invested roughly $120 billion

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:25.400
in similar short term work schemes.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:27.500
Since those employees
are still on the books,

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:28.770
even if they're not working,

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:31.210
they're not counted as unemployed.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:33.040
That works well for those lucky enough

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:34.750
to have a permanent job,

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:37.860
but it comes at the cost
of very high unemployment

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.860
for young people in some countries

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.530
or those on temporary contracts.

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:44.570
- I speak to people on furlough

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:48.360
and yeah, it's essentially a holiday.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:49.840
Obviously there's nervousness

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:52.040
that is concerned about the pandemic,

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:54.530
but people are spending
their time doing sports

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:55.680
or meeting the friends.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:56.960
It's a very different experience

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:01.190
being without work in Europe
at the moment than in the U.S.

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:04.160
- [Reporter] But the question
is, how long can it last?

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:07.390
- The labor cost is only part
of what companies are paying.

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:08.920
But you still have to
pay for the premises,

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.270
and there's other fees
that you have to pay,

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:12.170
and you've got no income.

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:15.340
So at some point you'll
still have to pull the plug

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:17.660
and let those employees go.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:18.890
- [Reporter] That breaking point

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:22.316
is going to depend on how
fast economies can recover.

00:04:22.316 --> 00:04:24.470
The longer the pandemic drags on,

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:26.490
the more companies could go bust,

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:30.590
erasing jobs that likely
won't be immediately replaced.

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:33.480
In June, the IMF projected
the global economy

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.970
to grow 5.4% next year,

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:38.630
but said recovery is still uncertain.

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:39.780
- In the next two or three years,

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:41.520
we'll see which response was better,

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:44.410
but traditionally the U.S. labor market

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:45.810
has been much more dynamic

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:49.200
in kind of reallocating
to growth industries.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:50.910
- [Reporter] Just look at
how the us stock market

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:53.720
has outperformed Japan and Europe.

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:56.310
Some big American companies
have done really well

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:57.680
throughout the pandemic.

00:04:57.680 --> 00:04:59.010
And there are high hopes

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:00.480
that the companies that have done well

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:02.140
will decide to create jobs

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:04.610
while Europe and Japan

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:08.010
have kept unemployment rates
relatively stable so far,

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:10.970
they risked supporting jobs
that might not be as valuable

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:13.030
by the time the pandemic ends.

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:16.190
Think aviation or German auto workers

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:19.253
producing parts for engines
that will likely be phased out.

00:05:19.253 --> 00:05:21.770
- Would you wanna have
exactly the same jobs in place

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:24.150
two years down the line? Probably not.

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:27.030
- [Reporter] Germany recently
allocated $12 billion

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:30.920
to expand its job support
program until the end of 2021.

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:34.970
But the future of us business
support is still unclear

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:36.510
as lawmakers have to regularly

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:38.883
assess and approve stimulus packages.

00:05:38.883 --> 00:05:40.261
- There's just so much uncertainty

00:05:40.261 --> 00:05:41.681
about how long this is gonna last.

00:05:41.681 --> 00:05:44.170
The next big debate is gonna
be about the exit strategy

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:47.920
and how to phase out
some of these programs.

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:49.730
- [Reporter] Even if the
U.S. unemployment rate

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:50.910
keeps falling,

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:53.540
the CBO predicts it'll
take more than a decade

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:55.890
to reach pre pandemic levels.

00:05:55.890 --> 00:05:57.440
While other developed countries

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:00.130
haven't made such
longterm projections yet,

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:03.580
the ECB forecast that
unemployment in the Eurozone

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:07.630
will still be above pre
pandemic levels in 2022.

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:09.910
A full economic recovery
from this pandemic

00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:11.660
is going to take years.

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:13.440
And by the time it's all over,

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.830
we may be looking at very
different labor markets

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:16.723
around the world.

